07:18:17Z +15s  | Runs store / state persistence                                                                                                                                                                                | `packages/cezar/src/runs/store.ts`                                                                                                                                                                                                                           | One embedded SQLite file per project, `.ai/cezar/runs.db` (`node:sqlite`, WAL; Node 24.15 is the floor and CI proves it), plus one append-only NDJSON event file per run. **Read first, in this order: `runs/run-database.ts` (the only SQL; schema, `transaction()`, claims, fenced writes), `runs/run-row.ts` (record to row: columns, summary, `isLiveRecord`), `runs/raw-record.ts` (keeps unknown fields across a write), `runs/store.ts` (`RunStore`, import, open), then `runs/run-index.ts` (read-only cold reader), `runs/legacy-index.ts` and `runs/store-open-error.ts`.** Each row holds the complete `RunRecord` JSON plus a stored `toRunSummary()` column and the few columns the store queries; `RunRecord` stays a zod schema, so new fields must still be optional or defaulted and rows written by an older cezar still parse. **Only the live set is in memory**: queued, running and waiting runs, runs a `RunManager` holds, pending wake timers and parents waiting on workers (`isLiveRecord`, plus `pin`). Finished runs load from the row on demand as fresh copies (frozen under vitest), so never keep or mutate one, and never rebuild a full scan of `runs`: add an indexed column. A delegation family is held, claimed and released as one unit (`run_claims`: session UUID, pid and start identity, checked inside the write transaction), so two processes on one project never both write a run. A foreign live run is readable and every control answers 409 `{ error }` (`requireRunControl`); `run_claims` and `run_conflicts` are privat